      "component_id": "ED26-P16-CH-finite-causal-models-C012",
      "verdict": "partial",
      "gap": "Pearl sec. 3.1, p. 106, inside the cited secs. 3.1-3.2.3, on the chain of Fig. 2(a): causal assumptions are untestable one at a time while their sum often has testable implications; the chain encodes seven causal assumptions, one per missing arrow or missing double arrow, which together imply that Z is unassociated with Y in every stratum of X, an implication read off the diagram by d-separation (Pearl 1988). The page then illustrates d-separation on the chain's diagram with its disturbances: U_Z -> Z -> X -> Y is blocked by {Z} and by {X}, giving the independencies printed as U_X indep Y | Z and U_Z indep Y | X for every parametrization; U_Z -> Z -> X <- U_X is blocked by the empty set but not by {Y}, so U_Z indep U_X holds while U_Z indep U_X | Y may fail; this collider behaviour is Berkson's paradox (Berkson 1946), with the example of two independent coins made dependent by the report that at least one shows a tail. It closes with the claim that d-separation independencies are the main opening through which such models face nonexperimental data, almost all tests able to invalidate the model being entailed by them, and its note 7 on dormant independences (Shpitser and Pearl 2008). None of this is in def:p16-causal-chain (body 692-733), its commentary (735-770) or elsewhere on the route: the chain's independence of Z and Y given X is never stated (thm:p16-finite-global-markov would yield it), and there is no illustration, collider or Berkson discussion. Only Definition 1 itself is treated, in def:p16-active-path, which cites p. 106 for that definition alone.",
